      "title": "त्वाम् (tvām) — you, one familiar person, as the object",
      "headword": "त्वाम्",
      "romanization": "tvām",
      "gloss": "you, one familiar person, as the object",
      "script": "devanagari",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:3ae28869a8b147e4",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say “he sees me”: सः माम् पश्यति (saḥ mām paśyati). Keep the frame and turn the object toward one familiar person."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: त्वाम्",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "त्वाम् (tvām) means “you” when one familiar addressee is the object."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "सः त्वाम् पश्यति। saḥ tvām paśyati. He sees you."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The opening tv- also appears in familiar tvam and tava. Here the long ā and final m belong to the independent accusative singular. Whitney §491 places त्वाम् (tvām) beside माम् (mām) in the first- and second-person paradigms."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "Taken Apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "As a grammatical form, त्वाम् (tvām) comes from the second-person pronoun paradigm; it is not a word from a separate lexical root. Whitney's Sanskrit Grammar §491 records it as the independent accusative singular. This lesson makes no deeper historical derivation claim."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Source: Whitney, Sanskrit Grammar (1889), §491."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "“he sees me” — saḥ mām paśyati",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: “he sees me” — *saḥ mām paśyati*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "“he sees you” — saḥ tvām paśyati",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: “he sees you” — *saḥ tvām paśyati*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "speaker as object — mām; one addressee as object — tvām",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: speaker as object — *mām*; one addressee as object — *tvām*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One familiar person is seen. Which object form addresses that person? (त्वाम्, tvām.)"
            }
          ]
        }
